{
  "about": "Settings for generate_unit_globals.py, one entry per unit (the main executable or an overlay). symbols: the symbol lists it takes names from. layout: its splat yaml, for its load address and image size, with {version} for us or jp; or vram and image_size given here. bss_end: where its BSS ends, if it has globals past its image. A setting that differs between the US and Japanese games is an object keyed by version. sources: its .c files, which are parsed for declarations and compiled. exclude: files in those the port replaces. defines: extra -D for the parse. aliases: names the code uses for addresses its symbol list names differently. containers: globals the code reads past the end of, with the symbol where the data they hold ends. separate: globals the PS1 lays inside another one that keep their own storage natively, because their native size differs (WMAP's packet buffers). skip: names defined elsewhere (the port, or the game's own source natively). overlay: false for the main executable. defines_by_file: -D renames for some of its files (a pattern, relative to game/), which route calls to the port: the pad, AKAO's lock and the SPU calls PSY-Z lacks. dropped_fields (for every unit): Type.member names the native build keeps elsewhere, not decoded (FieldItemStaging.record, whose block keeps its PS1 layout for the script variables). unions (for every unit): unions of a small number or a pointer, by type name, with the value below which a word is the number. runtime: globals the code builds itself (GPU frames), zeroed on load; their bytes in the file must be zero. element_decoders: arrays that also get a one-element decoder, lom_unit_decode_element_<name>, for the native build's views of what the PS1 reads past their ends (game/src/overlays/field/actors/field_target_views.c). hosts: overlays that load beside this one, whose lists name some of its data.",
  "dropped_fields": [
    "FieldItemStaging.record"
  ],
